.TH tr 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
tr \- translate, squeeze or delete characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tr
[\fB\-cds\fR] [\fIstring1\fR [\fIstring2\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tr
copies standard input to standard output, replacing each character found
in
.I string1
with the character in the same position in
.IR string2 .
It reads no files; give it a pipe or a redirect.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Use the complement of
.IR string1 :
every character that is
.I not
in it.
.TP
.B \-d
Delete the characters in
.I string1
rather than translating them.
.TP
.B \-s
Squeeze: replace each run of the same output character with one of it.
.SH STRINGS
A range is written in brackets:
.B [a\-z]
means the characters from
.B a
to
.BR z .
This is the older syntax, and the brackets are not optional.  A bare
.B a\-z
is three characters \- an
.BR a ,
a hyphen and a
.B z
\- so
.B tr a\-z A\-Z
turns
.B abc
into
.BR Abc .
It does not complain; it quietly does something else.
.PP
.BI \e ooo
is a character by octal code, and
.BI \e c
is
.I c
itself \- the way to include a bracket or a backslash.
.PP
There are no
.B [:alpha:]
style classes and no
.B [c*n]
repeats.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# tr [a-z] [A-Z] < file
# tr -d [0-9] < file
# tr -s " " < file
# tr [a-zA-Z] [n-za-mN-ZA-M] < file
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR cut (1)
